{"type": "FeatureCollection", "metadata": {"note": "Cincinnati's first railroad, and the answer to 'when did Cincinnati get passenger rail': the Little Miami Railroad, incorporated March 11 1836 (Ohio's second-ever railroad charter), with regular operations beginning by December 1841 (initial rails reached Milford) and the full Cincinnati-Springfield main line completed August 10 1846 -- nearly a decade before the Cincinnati-Dayton line depicted in big_four_cincinnati_cleveland.geojson, and 14+ years before 1860. This is the same physical corridor (Cincinnati-Xenia) this project traced once before under the wrong company name -- an earlier version of big_four_cincinnati_cleveland.geojson mistakenly used this alignment for the Big Four/New York Central, before a correction (prompted by a user noticing the route skipped Dayton) established that this Xenia routing actually belonged to a rival company, the Little Miami Railroad / Columbus & Xenia Railroad, later absorbed into the Pennsylvania Railroad system. That same real-traced OpenStreetMap geometry is reused here for its rightful owner.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little_Miami_Railroad (incorporated Mar 11 1836; rails reached Milford Dec 1841, regular operations began; completed to Springfield Aug 10 1846, 84 route-miles; merged with the Columbus and Xenia Railroad Nov 30 1853, creating the first through Cincinnati-Columbus rail route; leased in perpetuity Feb 23 1870 to the Pittsburgh, Cincinnati and St. Louis Railway, part of the Pennsylvania Railroad system; through Cincinnati-Xenia service suspended 1974; LMRR absorbed into Conrail Dec 23 1981)", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Columbus_and_Xenia_Railroad (the Columbus-Xenia portion of the merged route)"], "not_yet_sourced": "Exact frequency/timetable for any era; the precise year passenger (as opposed to through freight) service ended -- 1974 is sourced for 'through service' generally, not confirmed as specifically the last passenger train.", "